[R0029] year=2026 | venue=2026 International Symposium on Networks, Computers and Communications, ISNCC 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Agentic Cybersecurity: Autonomous Threat Detection Defense Against Adversarial Metamorphic Malware Attacks
KEYWORDS: Agentic AI; Cybersecurity; Large Language Models; Markov Decision Process; Metamorphic Malware; Multi-Agent Systems; Autonomous agents; Behavioral research; Benchmarking; Cryptography; Cybersecurity; Intelligent agents; Malware; Markov processes; Network security; Agentic AI; Cyber security; Language model; Large language model; Malware attacks; Malwares; Markov Decision Processes; Metamorphic malware; Multiagent systems (MASs); Threat detection; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: Adversarial malware evolves faster than traditional defenses can adapt. With the average breach costing $4.88 million in 2024 [1] and metamorphic malware exceeding 95% mutation rates, organizations need new approaches to detection and response. This paper presents a framework for applying agentic AI to security operations, introducing a multi-agent architecture for metamorphic malware detection that reaches 94.2% accuracy on known families against 8.7% recall for signaturebased systems. We report a pilot on 2,847 samples across five families, and a leave-one-family-out evaluation in which recall falls to 76.2% on unseen families-a bound we state explicitly, since the higher figure does not characterise performance against novel threats. Our implementation uses the ReAct paradigm with cryptographically secured agent communication (Ed25519 signatures, AES-256-GCM encryption). Grounded in Markov Decision Processes, we discuss practical deployment of agentic AI in enterprise security. Industry benchmarks report 80-87% response-time improvements; our framework has not yet been validated in a production SOC. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0030] year=2025 | venue=2025 International Conference on Innovation and Intelligence for Informatics, Computing,and Technologies, 3ICT 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Toward a Centralized Solution for Aggregating Penetration Testing and Risk Mitigation∗
KEYWORDS: Cybersecurity; Centralised; Cyber security; Decisions makings; Language model; Multi-Sources; Non-technical users; Organisational; Penetration Risk; Penetration testing; Risk mitigation; Decision making
ABSTRACT: As cybersecurity threats escalate, organizations struggle to track vulnerabilities and manage them efficiently. Existing tools produce fragmented reports that complicate decision-making for both technical and non-technical users. This research proposes a centralized, dockerized solution that consolidates multi-source vulnerability data into a single interface. The platform integrates multiple vulnerability scanning tools to analyze specific IP addresses, unifies their outputs, and leverages containerization for scalable deployment. By incorporating a local Large Language Model (LLM), the system automatically interprets scan results, generates AI-driven reports, and provides personalized recommendations tailored to organizational roles. A customizable dashboard further supports visualization and monitoring, bridging the gap between technical findings and executive decision-making. The solution was evaluated through experimental scans that demonstrated successful tool integration, accurate data consolidation, and effective generation of context-aware reports. Leveraging Large Language Models (LLMs), the solution generates tailored recommendations and training to address specific security issues. Designed for adaptability and scalability, the proposed system contributes a practical framework that enhances vulnerability management, reduces response times, and improves overall organizational resilience against evolving cybersecurity threats. © 2025 IEEE.

