TITLE: AI Agent-Assisted Lightweight Intrusion Detection for IoT Devices
KEYWORDS: 1DResNet; AI Agents; Edge Computing; Generative AI; Internet of Things (IoT); Intrusion Detection System (IDS); Lightweight Deep Learning; Complex networks; Computer crime; Deep learning; Edge detection; Intelligent agents; Internet of things; Intrusion detection; Network security; 1dresnet; Artificial intelligence agent; Edge computing; Generative artificial intelligence; Internet of thing; Intrusion detection system; Intrusion Detection Systems; Intrusion-Detection; Lightweight deep learning; Traffic volumes; Edge computing
ABSTRACT: The rapid increase in Internet of Things (IoT) devices has raised serious security concerns, mainly due to growing traffic volumes and more complex cyber threats. At the same time, many existing intrusion detection systems (IDS) are not well suited for resource-constrained edge environments. In this study, a lightweight IDS framework supported by an Artificial Intelligence (AI) agent is introduced for such settings. The system is built around a compact 1D-ResNet model with 28,759 parameters (approximately $\mathbf{1 2 7 K B}$), allowing multiclass attack detection with low computational cost. Rather than focusing only on accuracy, the model is designed to operate as a fast pre-filter. It scans network traffic and identifies suspicious flows at an early stage, so that more detailed analysis can be handled by more complex models. This method is scalable and supports layered IDS designs. Proposed model trained on the CIC-IoT-2023 dataset, which has a significant class imbalance. To overcome this imbalance problem, the Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique (SMOTE) was applied to improve the detection of minority attack types. In addition, a cloud-based AI Security Analyst agent powered by Gemini 2.5 Flash converts model outputs and metadata into structured, human-readable reports. Lightweight 1D-ResNet model performed 82.84% accuracy, while keeping computational overhead low. These results demonstrate that the proposed approach is suitable for real-time edge deployment. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0034] year=2025 | venue=Proceedings - 2025 IEEE 3rd International Symposium on Sustainable Energy, Signal Processing and Cybersecurity, iSSSC 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Network Penetration Testing: A Comprehensive Review of Tools, Vulnerabilities, and AI-Driven Solutions
KEYWORDS: AI in cybersecurity; Automated Pentesting; Network Penetration Testing; Penetration Testing Tools; Vulnerability Assessment; Artificial intelligence; Cybersecurity; Ethical aspects; Software testing; Artificial intelligence in cybersecurity; Automated pentesting; Cyber security; Intelligence models; Misconfigurations; Modeling architecture; Network penetration testing; Penetration testing tools; Simple++; Vulnerability assessments; Network security
ABSTRACT: Network penetration Testing (or pentesting) is a positive way to find vulnerabilities associated with the increasingly complex world of IoT, Cloud, and 5G. In this discussion paper, we examine the evolution of declining penetration testing tools, from simple tools such as Nmap and Metasploit, to the emergence of sophisticated Artificial Intelligence (AI)-powered platforms like Darktrace and GuardiCore. We discuss the categories of known vulnerabilities, including misconfiguration, weak authentication, and outdated software, and how different AI model architectures, particularly reinforcement learning and large language models, can be leveraged to address components of the mission, such as automating reconnaissance, identifying vulnerable systems, and generating exploitation vectors. Although AI will increase speed, scale, and ease of use, challenges such as trust, explainability, and robustness against adversarial attacks remain significant hurdles. We underscore the necessity of a hybrid, human-in-the-loop model where AI augments human expertise, a framework often mandated by compliance and ethical considerations. In this respect, we identify the need for a strong explainability framework and a consideration of ethical issues and auditability aspects throughout any automated testing framework. Lastly, we suggest future planning for research and development into kinds of autonomous (self- learning) frameworks that will grow with changing adversarial threats using AI, while also lamenting the diminishing role of humans to augment (collaborate with) AI, in what is an important field of cybersecurity. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0035] year=2025 | venue=13th International Conference on Learning Representations, ICLR 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: CYBENCH: A FRAMEWORK FOR EVALUATING CYBERSECURITY CAPABILITIES AND RISKS OF LANGUAGE MODELS
KEYWORDS: 
ABSTRACT: Language Model (LM) agents for cybersecurity that are capable of autonomously identifying vulnerabilities and executing exploits have potential to cause real-world impact. Policymakers, model providers, and researchers in the AI and cybersecurity communities are interested in quantifying the capabilities of such agents to help mitigate cyberrisk and investigate opportunities for penetration testing. Toward that end, we introduce Cybench, a framework for specifying cybersecurity tasks and evaluating agents on those tasks. We include 40 professional-level Capture the Flag (CTF) tasks from 4 distinct CTF competitions, chosen to be recent, meaningful, and spanning a wide range of difficulties. Each task includes its own description, starter files, and is initialized in an environment where an agent can execute commands and observe outputs. Since many tasks are beyond the capabilities of existing LM agents, we introduce subtasks for each task, which break down a task into intermediary steps for a more detailed evaluation. To evaluate agent capabilities, we construct a cybersecurity agent and evaluate 8 models: GPT-4o, OpenAI o1-preview, Claude 3 Opus, Claude 3.5 Sonnet, Mixtral 8x22b Instruct, Gemini 1.5 Pro, Llama 3 70B Chat, and Llama 3.1 405B Instruct. For the top performing models (GPT-4o and Claude 3.5 Sonnet), we further investigate performance across 4 agent scaffolds (structured bash, action-only, pseudoterminal, and web search). Without subtask guidance, agents leveraging Claude 3.5 Sonnet, GPT-4o, OpenAI o1-preview, and Claude 3 Opus successfully solved complete tasks that took human teams up to 11 minutes to solve. In comparison, the most difficult task took human teams 24 hours and 54 minutes to solve. © 2025 13th International Conference on Learning Representations, ICLR 2025. All rights reserved.

