ABSTRACT: Security operators use red teams to simulate real attackers and proactively find defense gaps. In realistic enterprise settings, this involves executing multi-host network attacks spanning many 'stepping stone' hosts. Unfortunately, red teams are expensive and entail significant expertise and effort. Given the promise of LLMs in CTF challenges, we first analyze whether LLMs can autonomously execute multi-host red-team exercises. We find that state-of-the-art LLM-assisted offense systems (e.g., PentestGPT, CyberSecEval3) with leading LLMs (e.g., Sonnet 4, Gemini 2.5 Pro) are unable to do so. Building on our observations in understanding the failure modes of state-of-the-art systems, we argue the need to improve the abstractions and interfaces for LLM-assisted red teaming. Based on this insight, we design and implement Incalmo11All code is publicly available: Incalmo GitHub an LLM-assisted system for autonomously red teaming multihost networks. Incalmo uses LLMs to plan red-team exercises in terms of high-level declarative tasks that are executed by domain-specific task agents. Incalmo also uses auxiliary services to manage context and acquired assets. For our evaluation, we develop MHBench, a novel multihost attack benchmark with 40 realistic emulated networks (from 22 to 50 hosts). We find that Incalmo successfully acquires critical assets (i.e., key hosts or data) in 37 of 40 MHBench environments. In contrast, state-of-the-art LLMassisted systems succeed in only three of 40 environments. We also show that Incalmo is efficient: successful attacks took 1254 minutes and cost ≤ 15 in LLM credits. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0041] year=2026 | venue=Proceedings of the International Symposium on Digital Forensics and Security, ISDFS | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Event-Driven Agentic SOC (ED-ASOC): Supervisor-Based LLM Framework for Dynamic Incident Response and SOAR Orchestration
KEYWORDS: Agentic AI; Digital Forensics; Human-in-the-Loop; Incident Response; Large Language Models; Retrieval-Augmented Generation; Security Operations Center; SOAR; Autonomous agents; Computer crime; Digital forensics; Electronic crime countermeasures; Network security; Risk management; Security systems; Agentic AI; Event-driven; Human-in-the-loop; Incident response; Language model; Large language model; Retrieval-augmented generation; Security operation center; SOAR; Supervisor agents; Supervisory personnel
ABSTRACT: As cyber threats grow more sophisticated, Security Operations Centers (SOC) face mounting pressure from the gap between alert volumes and the speed of traditional incident response. Analysts struggle with cognitive overload caused by high false-positive rates, leading to alert fatigue and missed critical threats. Current SOAR platforms, despite automating routine tasks, rely on polling-based data retrieval and static playbooks that cannot adapt to polymorphic or zero-day attacks. This paper presents the Event-Driven Agentic SOC (ED-ASOC) framework, built around a Supervisor Agent that enables the shift from scripted automation to autonomous orchestration. By replacing periodic polling with Webhookbased real-time ingestion, the framework cuts detection-toanalysis latency from minutes to sub-second levels. The Supervisor Agent enriches raw telemetry with organizational context through Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), producing adaptive responses that move beyond rigid rules. The agent can execute remediation actions, including IP blocking, endpoint isolation, and user suspension, via defined API schemas, closing the gap between detection and containment. A tiered Human-in-the-Loop (HITL) mechanism ensures that high-impact decisions require analyst approval. Experimental evaluation using FortiSIEM and FortiSOAR in a controlled testbed demonstrates a 73% reduction in Mean Time to Response (MTTR) and 89% decrease in detection latency compared to polling-based baselines, while reducing Tier-1 analyst workload by approximately 45%. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0042] year=2026 | venue=2026 International Conference on System, Computation, Automation and Networking, ICSCAN 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Self-Reasoning Language Models for Hallucination-Resistant Cyber Threat Intelligence Ecosystems
KEYWORDS: Cyber Threat Intelligence; Explainable AI; Hallucination Detection; Large Language Models; Retrieval-Augmented Generation; Self-Reflective Language Models; Trustworthy AI; Computational linguistics; Cybersecurity; Learning systems; Multi agent systems; Network security; Reliability; Cybe threat intelligence; Cyber threats; Explainable AI; Hallucination detection; Language model; Large language model; Retrieval-augmented generation; Self-reflective language model; Trustworthy AI; Iterative methods
ABSTRACT: Automated Cyber Threat Intelligence (CTI) generation through the usage of large language models (LLMs) will continue to grow as they are being adopted for use in the field of Cybersecurity. At the same time, the issue of hallucinations produced by these systems continues to be a major concern when it comes to producing accurate and credible information regarding specific threats. This paper presents an entirely new Self- Reflective Cyber Threat Intelligence (SR-CTI) framework designed to improve the reliability and credibility of the intelligence generated by automated CTIs. The SR-CTI framework incorporates two main components: retrieval- based evidence grounding method for detecting and correcting hallucinations, as well as a self-reflective critique and iterative refining process that addresses both. Experimental results show that the SR-CTI framework will improve the factual precision of factual content accuracy by approximately 28%, evidence coverage by approximately 25%, and averaged rates of hallucination will be reduced by approximately 74%, as compared to baseline LLMs. Improvements in internal consistency and attribution reliability were 18% to 22% respectively (large positive changes). Advanced evaluations (ROC, Precision/ Recall, and calibration) confirm excellent performance and well- calibrated confidence estimations. This shows how adding self- reflection and verification (loop) elements to simple ('one-pass') generation provides a comprehensive, trust- savvy Intelligence ('I') pipeline. The proposed 'Future Work' Framework presents a practical means by which to deploy LLM's into realistic cybersecurity environments where precise results and reliability are paramount. Future efforts will focus on validating multiple agents (MAs) and adapting to changing attack vectors via machine learning technologies. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0043] year=2026 | venue=Proceedings of IEEE/IFIP Network Operations and Management Symposium, NOMS 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: ALIoTh: Orchestrating Multi-Agent LLMs for Autonomous IoT Penetration Testing
