ABSTRACT: Penetration testing in Internet of Things (IoT) environments, especially those composed of various different devices, e.g., smart plugs, smart cameras, and smart doors, presents great challenges across the phases of information gathering, attack surface enumeration, privilege escalation, and lateral movement. This paper introduces ALIoTh, a multi-agent, workflow-driven penetration testing framework that integrates Large Language Models agents within a Self-Retrieval-Augmented Generation (Self-RAG) architecture to autonomously identify and exploit vulnerabilities in IoT and Industrial-IoT (IIoT) environments. By leveraging state-of-the-art language models and orchestrating specialized agents via the LangGraph framework, ALIoTh dynamically plans and executes a full-spectrum offensive pipeline. This includes network, devices, and service enumeration, vulnerability assessment, payload generation, reverse shell deployment, and privilege escalation on each device. The Self-RAG mechanism equips expert agents with contextual access to IoT security knowledge, e.g., related documentation, tool manuals, and known attack techniques, enhancing their reasoning capabilities in scenarios involving multiple device exploitation and group policy manipulation. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0044] year=2026 | venue=Computers | type=Article
TITLE: Intelligent Attack Detection in Blockchain-Enabled Multi-Cloud Systems: A Systematic Review and SOC-LLM-Augmented Architecture Proposal
KEYWORDS: augmented SOC; blockchain; LLM; multi-cloud security; sophisticated attack; systematic review; Anomaly detection; Architecture; Authentication; Autonomous agents; Cloud computing architecture; Cloud security; Cloud storage; Computer architecture; Digital libraries; Intelligent agents; Learning systems; Network security; System-on-chip; Attack detection; Augmented SOC; Block-chain; Cloud securities; Cloud systems; LLM; Multi-cloud security; Multi-clouds; Sophisticated attack; Systematic Review; Blockchain
ABSTRACT: This paper presents a systematic literature review examining how blockchain technologies can enhance the security and performance of multi-cloud systems. Multi-cloud architectures offer resilience, scalability, and flexibility; however, they also pose complex security challenges related to APIs, service-level agreements (SLAs), orchestration, and authentication. The promise of blockchain technology to improve the security and transparency of numerous applications, including cloud storage systems, has attracted considerable attention in recent years. Much research has focused on decentralized storage in cloud environments, spanning supply chains, FinTech, healthcare, and education. Still, the integration of blockchain with the cloud and its potential to enhance security and performance warrant an in-depth study. Using the PRISMA methodology, a structured search was conducted across six major scientific databases, including IEEE, ACM Digital Library, ScienceDirect, Scopus, Web of Science, and IJIMAI. Twenty-four primary papers published between 2019 and 2025 were selected for analysis after clear inclusion and exclusion criteria were applied. This review examines the security dimensions in multi-cloud environments—architectural vulnerabilities, API security, authentication, orchestration and automation vulnerabilities, SLAs, and cybersecurity compliance issues—in relation to blockchain technology. Based on the identified gaps, we propose a SOC-LLM-augmented security architecture that integrates blockchain-based evidence integrity, statistical anomaly detection, machine learning, large language models, and autonomous AI agents to enable intelligent attack detection and response. The proposed framework introduces specialized agents for detection, correlation, threat intelligence retrieval, blockchain evidence validation, explanation generation, and response planning. The analysis shows that integrating SOC-LLM capabilities with blockchain can move multi-cloud security from passive auditability toward proactive, explainable, and human-in-the-loop cyber defense. Finally, this paper discusses open challenges, including LLM hallucination, data scarcity, real-time scalability, evaluation standardization, and trustworthy deployment in critical multi-cloud infrastructures. The study’s conclusion highlights research gaps and suggests future lines of inquiry concerning scalable blockchain architectures and the incorporation of AI for proactive cloud security monitoring. © 2026 by the authors.

[R0045] year=2025 | venue=2025 5th International Conference on Emerging Research in Electronics, Computer Science and Technology, ICERECT 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Cognitive Autonomy for Machine-Centric IoT: A Foundation-Model Blueprint for Secure, Real-Time Automation at the Edge
KEYWORDS: Autonomous Systems; Edge Intelligence; Foundation Models; Internet of Things (IoT); Resilient Control; Agricultural machinery; Anomaly detection; Automation; Autonomous agents; Cognitive systems; Data privacy; Edge computing; Firmware; Intelligent agents; Internet of things; Network security; Precision agriculture; Robust control; Transfer learning; Autonomous system; Edge intelligence; Foundation models; Generalisation; Heterogeneous devices; Internet of thing; Model-driven; Real-time automation; Resilient control; Roadmap; Budget control
ABSTRACT: This paper proposes a machine-centric roadmap for adopting artificial intelligence in Internet-of-Things (IoT) automation, emphasizing foundation-model-driven cognition deployed across resource-constrained, heterogeneous devices. We articulate a layered architecture that unifies multimodal perception (sensors, telemetry, and environmental signals), adaptive actuation, and memory-augmented reasoning to deliver robust control under distribution shifts and incomplete data. The framework integrates low-latency model compression (pruning, quantization) with on-device inference, selective edge-cloud offloading, and retrieval-augmented decision loops to achieve real-time performance without sacrificing generalization. To address the realities of industrial machine networks, we delineate domain generalization and transfer-learning strategies for temporal drift, multi-source variability, and scarce labels, alongside privacy-preserving and attack-resilient mechanisms spanning firmware hardening, trusted execution, and anomaly detection. Cross-domain use cases - from converters in smart grids and intelligent motor drives to precision agriculture, healthcare wearables, and cooperative transportation - demonstrate how AGI-inspired capabilities (planning, multimodal understanding, tool use, and few-shot adaptation) can elevate IoT from siloed analytics to resilient, closed-loop autonomy. We conclude with implementation guidelines that balance energy budgets, bandwidth constraints, and safety, positioning AI-first IoT machines as continuously learning agents that are secure, interoperable, and ready for large-scale, real-world automation. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0046] year=2026 | venue=IEEE Access | type=Article
TITLE: Decision Accountability in LLM-Driven UAVs: A Privacy-Preserving Forensic Logging Framework
KEYWORDS: Autonomous systems; cyber-physical security; decision accountability; forensic logging; large language models; privacy preservation; unmanned aerial vehicles; Abstracting; Antennas; Behavioral research; Benchmarking; Chains; Computation theory; Decision making; Digital forensics; Digital storage; Distributed computer systems; Forensic engineering; Network security; Privacy-preserving techniques; Safety engineering; Telemetering; Telemetering equipment; Uncertainty analysis; Aerial vehicle; Autonomous system; Cyber-Physical securities; Decision accountability; Forensic logging; Language model; Large language model; Model-driven; Privacy preservation; Unmanned aerial vehicle; Unmanned aerial vehicles (UAV)
ABSTRACT: Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly being integrated into unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) systems to support mission planning, policy interpretation, safety assessment, and cyber-physical decision making. However, decisions generated by LLM-guided UAVs are often ephemeral, while conventional UAV logs mainly preserve telemetry, sensor measurements, control traces, and command records. These artifacts do not ordinarily preserve a structured and verifiable record linking a discrete decision to its retained context, selected action, temporal position, and integrity evidence. This limits accountability, forensic traceability, and regulatory auditability in privacy-sensitive operational environments. This paper proposes a privacy-preserving forensic logging framework for LLM-driven UAV decision systems. The framework introduces a compact DecisionRecord abstraction that captures decision semantics, temporal ordering, and integrity metadata in a model-interface-agnostic form. It also defines a constrained DecisionRecord′ representation under the P2 profile, excluding raw prompts, unrestricted reasoning, telemetry streams, precise locations, and complete candidate-choice lists while retaining selected evidence for post-incident investigation. Privacy preservation is operationalized as bounded data minimization rather than anonymization, PII detection, formal privacy protection, or resistance to inferential disclosure. Evidentiary integrity is protected using deterministic canonical serialization, SHA-256 hash chaining, and trusted checkpoint comparison. Evaluation using 200 deterministically sampled UAVBench instances produced 0/800 whole-field verbatim retention for the evaluated omitted source fields, 1,400/1,400 predefined evidence-group availability, and a 49.224% reduction in average canonical payload size relative to the canonically serialized source MCQs. Local verification detected all 20/20 independently executed record modifications and all 5/5 interior deletions. All five coherently recomputed suffixes passed local verification but were detected through mismatch with an independently retained trusted terminal checkpoint. Across 30 workstation executions, mean end-to-end record-construction latency was 0.563214 ms per record, and the complete 200-record chain occupied 220.795 KiB. The results establish benchmark-grounded feasibility for compact, data-minimized, and tamper-evident preservation of discrete LLM-guided UAV decision events. They do not establish decision accuracy, authenticated model identity, recovery of hidden reasoning, complete mission reconstruction, or onboard UAV performance. © 2013 IEEE.

[R0047] year=2025 | venue=8th Conference on Cloud and Internet of Things, CIoT 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Autonomous and Context-Adaptive Defense Mechanisms for Identity Access Management in Internet of Medical Things
KEYWORDS: Adaptive Security; Anomaly Detection; Autonomous Defense; Cybersecurity; Identity and Access Management (IAM); Internet of Medical Things (IoMT); Large Language Models (LLMs); Application programming interfaces (API); Cybersecurity; Engines; Internet of things; Internet protocols; Network security; Adaptive security; Anomaly detection; Autonomous defense; Cyber security; Identity and access management; Identity and access managements; Internet of medical thing; Language model; Large language model; Real- time; Anomaly detection
ABSTRACT: Identity and Access Management (IAM) in the Internet of Medical Things (IoMT) faces critical challenges, including the detection of sophisticated attack patterns and the execution of real-Time threat responses. This work presents the IoMT Sentinel Platform, an adaptive security framework designed for IoMT environments. The platform integrates a finetuned DistilBERT model for anomaly detection in contextualized IAM and system logs, achieving an accuracy of 99.98 % on a simulated IoMT dataset. Beyond detection, the platform introduces a dynamic trust scoring engine that evaluates each device's security posture based on access history, network behavior, and anomaly records. This trust score directly influences an automated response engine that leverages Keycloak APIs to revoke tokens, adjust privileges, and block malicious IP addresses in real time. Experimental evaluations across multiple scenarios demonstrate the system's ability to detect volumetric and distributed attacks, enforce dynamic trust-based policies, and adaptively mitigate threats with low response latency. This work is among the first to integrate a lightweight language model, a dynamic trust scoring engine, and an autonomous IAM mitigation system into a unified IoMT security framework. Unlike previous approaches that focus solely on detection, the proposed platform emphasizes real-Time defense through seamless coupling of anomaly detection and IAM policy enforcement. Our results demonstrate that such a synergy enables proactive and scalable protection in medical IoT environments. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0048] year=2026 | venue=2026 6th International Conference on Artificial Intelligence, Big Data and Algorithms, CAIBDA 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: A Hallucination Detection Framework for Large Language Models in Cybersecurity: Design and Implementation
KEYWORDS: Cybersecurity; Hallucination Detection; Large Language Model; Penetration Testing; Self-Consistency; Cybersecurity; Data visualization; Life cycle; Statistical tests; Visualization; Cyber security; Design and implementations; Detection framework; Detection methods; Hallucination detection; Language model; Large language model; Operational security; Penetration testing; Self-consistency; Software testing
ABSTRACT: While Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly integrated into cybersecurity penetration testing, their inherent hallucination issues severely threaten operational security. Existing hallucination detection methods primarily target general domains and heavily rely on external knowledge retrieval, making them inherently unsuitable for penetration testing scenarios that typically operate within intranets. This paper proposes a self-consistency hallucination detection framework tailored for penetration testing. Specifically, we first construct a hallucination evaluation dataset based on the MITRE ATT&CK framework, covering knowledge of tactics, techniques, and software throughout the penetration testing lifecycle. Second, we optimize the general self-consistency detection method and validate its effectiveness on this dataset, achieving an F1-score of 0.77 and a recall of 0.85. Finally, we develop CyberChat, a web-based tool that implements the optimized self-consistency hallucination detection method and features an interactive visualization interface. This study provides an initial exploration of LLM hallucination detection in the cybersecurity domain, offering technical support for the reliable application of LLMs in high-risk scenarios. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0049] year=2026 | venue=2026 International Conference on Sustainable Computing and Integrated Communication in Changing Landscape of Al, ICSCAI 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: An Intelligent Large Language Model Driven Neurosymbolic Architecture for Autonomous Cyber Threat Identification and Containment
KEYWORDS: Autonomous Containment; Cyber Threat Detection; Dynamic Isolation Mesh; Large Language Models; Neurosymbolic Reasoning; Self-Healing Security Systems; Threat Capsule Encoding; Architecture; Artificial intelligence; Computer architecture; Malware; Security systems; Zero-day attack; Autonomous containment; Cybe threat detection; Cyber threats; Dynamic isolation; Dynamic isolation mesh; Encodings; Language model; Large language model; Neurosymbolic reasoning; Self-healing; Self-healing security system; Threat capsule encoding; Threat detection; Self-healing materials
ABSTRACT: The fast-growing capabilities of cyber threats that include automated malware, AI-produced exploits, and multi-stage intrusions organized by coordinating defense measures have rendered traditional defense systems less effective. Current security designs are largely and mostly detection based and rely on human observers to interpret, give threat prioritization, and execution of responses. This kind of manual intervention creates some delays that can be used by opponents to increase privileges, side-step, or install persistence. In this study, a smart LLM-based neurosymbolic architecture to identify and contain autonomously cyber threats will be proposed, which can think in real-time and context using intelligent reasoning and a micro-level level of response. This model combines three new technologies: (1) Threat Capsule Generator: compact, high-signal representation units produced by the transformation has to be transduced into raw logs; (2) Hybrid NeuralSymbolic Reasoning Loop: the deep contextualised understanding is to be balanced with the deterministic assurance of policy; and (3) Dynamic Isolation Mesh: micro-segments containment domains are to be created in mainland to restrict the spread of malicious processes without interrupting normal business activity. The architecture also includes an Explainability Layer which provides autonomous integrity of the system and a Self-Healing Module which provides transparent auditability and trust. There are experimental assessments of the proposed system with real and synthetic attack data which prove that their system causes a reduction in containment latency by 70 percent, as well as increases the zero-day detection rates by 40 percent and sustains greater than 98 percent system uptime in the active mitigation conditions. The findings affirm the viability and effectiveness of autonomous containment with the help of the LLM, which is an important step in the next-generation cyber defence. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0050] year=2024 | venue=Lecture Notes in Networks and Systems | type=Conference paper
TITLE: An Approach Based on LLMs for Forensic Threat Detection in Autonomous Systems
KEYWORDS: Cybersecurity; Explainability; Forensics; LLM; Robotics; ROS 2; Crime; Cyber attacks; Electronic crime countermeasures; Forensic engineering; Mobile robots; Cyber security; Cyber-attacks; Explainability; Forensic; Human-systems; LLM; Robotic systems; ROS 2; Security measure; Threat detection; Denial-of-service attack
ABSTRACT: In a world where interaction between humans and robotic systems is increasingly common, cybersecurity is essential to protect the integrity of both. Security measures to protect humans from possible accidents or cyber-attacks on a robotic system are essential. However, after an unexpected event, whether caused by a system malfunction or the influence of a malicious actor, it must be possible to explain what happened. In this paper, we propose to combine the fields of explainability and cybersecurity by exploring the potential of an explainability system in robotics based on LLMs as a forensic tool. We performed a denial of service attack on the mobile robot’s controller. After, we analyzed the logs generated by the robot using the proposed explainability system. The results obtained show that this system can detect the presence of a potential threat affecting the robot’s behavior. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2024.

[R0051] year=2025 | venue=CSAE 2025 - Proceedings of the 9th International Conference on Computer Science and Application Engineering | type=Conference paper
TITLE: AutoPen: Towards Autonomous Penetration Testing Using LLM-Powered Agents
KEYWORDS: Large language models; LLM; LLM agent; penetration testing; RAG; Agents; Artificial intelligence; Automation; Autonomous agents; Computation theory; Distributed computer systems; Network security; Open source software; Open systems; Language model; Large language model; Large language model agent; Model agents; Penetration testing; Retrieval-augmented generation; System security; Computational methods
ABSTRACT: Penetration testing is an effective means of ensuring system security, yet it suffers from drawbacks such as being time-consuming, labor-intensive, and requiring highly specialized operators. The advancement of large language models (LLMs) has made it feasible to utilize them for automated penetration testing. In this paper, we propose AutoPen, a method designed to autonomously conduct penetration testing without human intervention. AutoPen employs techniques such as Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), chain-of-Thought reasoning, and structured output to obtain more relevant and accurate responses from the LLM. Furthermore, it incorporates a carefully designed penetration testing workflow with task decomposition and specialized agents to ensure efficient and autonomous operation. We conducted penetration testing experiments on various vulnerable hosts from VulnHub. The results demonstrate that AutoPen performs automated penetration testing with high stability and exhibits significantly superior capability compared to open-source methods and other approaches described in related literature. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).

[R0052] year=2026 | venue=Applied Sciences (Switzerland) | type=Article
TITLE: A Hybrid Multi-Agent System for Early Scam Detection in Crypto-Assets
KEYWORDS: compliance automation; crypto-asset markets; explainable AI; fraud detection; large language models; markets in Crypto-Assets Regulation (MiCAR); multi-agent systems; off-chain due diligence; regulatory technology (RegTech); Automation; Blockchain; Building components; Compliance control; Crime; Data acquisition; Intelligent agents; Learning systems; Network security; Asset markets; Compliance automation; Crypto-asset market; Due diligence; Explainable AI; Fraud detection; Language model; Large language model; Market in crypto-asset regulation; Multiagent systems (MASs); Off-chain due diligence; Regulatory technology; Article; artificial intelligence; blockchain; ChatGPT; crypto asset; cryptocurrency; data source; deep learning; document analysis; documentation; early scam detection; fraud; graph neural network; hybrid; information processing; large language model; machine learning; maturity; nonhuman; quantitative analysis; questionnaire; reproducibility; workflow; Multi agent systems; Regulatory compliance
ABSTRACT: The rapid expansion of crypto-asset markets and the introduction of the Markets in Crypto-Assets Regulation (MiCAR) pose novel supervisory challenges. Existing blockchain intelligence platforms focus predominantly on on-chain surveillance, leaving gaps in off-chain documentary due diligence automation. This paper presents a Multi-Agent System (MAS) integrating Large Language Model (LLM) capabilities with rule-based compliance frameworks. The architecture comprises seven specialized agents: a Coordinator Agent for orchestration; data acquisition agents (Searcher, Crawler); three parallel analytical agents—Heuristic Agent (LLM-powered qualitative risk assessment), Compliance Agent (hybrid-AI MiCAR asset classification and regulatory requirement verification), and On-Chain Agent (machine learning-based fraud detection); and a Reconciliator Agent synthesizing findings into unified alerts. Component-level empirical validation on 150 projects indicates 95% output reproducibility (identical alert tier and score deviation (Formula presented.) across five reruns) and 210 s mean latency, providing proof-of-concept evidence for the integrated pipeline. A pilot user evaluation (six researchers/master students and two experts from regulatory authorities) provides preliminary usability evidence and surfaces domain-specific feedback from regulatory-authority experts. The architecture advances proactive regulatory technology by enabling scalable analysis combining off-chain documentary evidence with on-chain forensics. © 2026 by the authors.

[R0053] year=2026 | venue=Proceedings of the IEEE International Conference on Computer Communication and the Internet, ICCCI | type=Conference paper
TITLE: GAI-AVRS: Generative AI-driven Autonomous Vulnerability Response System
KEYWORDS: Attack Graph; Generative Artificial Intelligence; Graph Attention Network; Large Language Model; Model Context Protocol; Artificial intelligence; Complex networks; Computation theory; Internet protocols; Network security; Remediation; Risk management; Safety engineering; Attack graph; Common vulnerability scoring systems; Generative artificial intelligence; Graph attention network; Language model; Large language model; Model context protocol; Model contexts; Path level; Response systems; Risk assessment
ABSTRACT: Traditional vulnerability response systems frequently rely on isolated vulnerability metrics, such as the Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS), to prioritize remediation efforts. However, these static scores fail to capture the cascading effects of attack propagation, lateral movement, and asset interdependencies, leading to inaccurate risk assessment in complex network environments. This paper proposes GAI-AVRS, an autonomous vulnerability response system that integrates generative AI, an Edge-aware Graph Attention Network (Edgeaware GAT), and Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG). GAI-AVRS constructs heterogeneous attack graphs from assets, vulnerabilities, components, and network connections; learns node and edge level risk representations; computes path level risk scores; and aggregates them into global vulnerability level remediation priorities. In the response stage, Large Language Models (LLMs) leverage Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVE) details and Model Context Protocol (MCP) Server/Tool retrieval results to generate mitigation reports with remediation order and mitigation procedures. Experiments on a MITRE ATT&CK informed dataset show that GAI-AVRS outperforms CVSS, Exploit Prediction Scoring System (EPSS), GPT based ranking, and random baselines in path-level and vulnerability level NDCG@K. For mitigation report generation, GPT-5.5 achieves the best overall quality in Precision, Recall, Embedding Cosine Similarity, and Answer Correctness. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0054] year=2026 | venue=Lecture Notes in Computer Science | type=Conference paper
TITLE: SecKQL-Agent: A Real-World APT29 Events Benchmark and Framework for Reliable Text-to-KQL in Security Analytics
KEYWORDS: Cybersecurity Analytics; Kusto Query Language (KQL); LLM-based Agent; Text-to-Query Generation; Natural language processing systems; Network security; Query languages; Query processing; Security systems; Semantics; Structured Query Language; Cyber security; Cybersecurity analytic; Kusto query language; Large-scales; LLM-based agent; Natural languages; Query generation; Real-world; Security Analytics; Text-to-query generation; Computational efficiency
ABSTRACT: Text-to-query language (Text-to-QL) techniques allow analysts to express complex threat-hunting and investigation intents in natural language, lowering the barrier to large-scale security telemetry and facilitating practical security analytics. Yet, despite this potential, public resources for this domain remain scarce due to the technical complexities involved in handling wide-column, semi-structured, and high-volume security datasets. To fill this gap, we construct SecKQL-APT29, a Kusto Query Language (KQL) benchmark derived from real APT29 attack traces. It offers an executable, structured schema and expert-annotated Text-to-KQL pairs. We also propose SecKQL-Agent, a Text-to-KQL framework tailored for security analytics that addresses limitations of directly applying Text-to-SQL methods: (i) redundant and oversized schemas, (ii) limited domain adaptation to security query languages, and (iii) semantic mismatch between generated queries and user intent. SecKQL-Agent comprises three components: a Hybrid-attention Schema Refiner for relevant and compact schema selection; an Adaptive Few-shot Generator for robust query generation; and a Chain-of-Thought (CoT)-driven Semantic Consistency Reflector for result-aware semantic validation. Experiments on two benchmarks against three representative baselines show that SecKQL-Agent achieves an optimal balance between execution accuracy and computational efficiency, demonstrating its effectiveness and generalizability. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Singapore Pte Ltd. 2026.

[R0055] year=2026 | venue=AAMAS 2026 - Proceedings of the 25th International Conference on Autonomous Agents and Multiagent Systems | type=Conference paper
TITLE: MAStitch: Unifying Local and Global Perspectives for Anomaly Detection in Multi-Agent Systems
KEYWORDS: Adversarial Attacks; Anomaly Detection; LLM-based Agents; Multi-Agent Systems; Security Analysis; Anomaly detection; Association reactions; Autonomous agents; Decision making; Intelligent agents; Network security; Anomaly detection; Decisions makings; Diverse applications; Global perspective; Language model; Large language model-based agent; Local perspective; Model-based OPC; Multiagent systems (MASs); Security analysis; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: Large language model (LLM)-based multi-agent systems (MASs) increasingly serve as decision-making and automation pipelines in diverse application domains. Although these systems are highly relied upon in many settings, they have been shown to be vulnerable to various threats, including threats exploited by malicious actors (e.g., adversarial attacks) and threats stemming from dangerous behavior of the system (e.g., insecure code generation). Those threats can be viewed as deviations from normal behavior (anomalies), requiring a robust anomaly detection solution. Most approaches for detecting such anomalies concentrate on individual agents, specific failure scenarios, or particular use cases, and often require additional training phases, limiting their applicability and ease of adoption across diverse MASs. To address these limitations, we introduce MAStitch, a platform- and threat-agnostic method for threat detection in MASs that does not require any training, making it well-suited for diverse environments and scenarios requiring plug-and-play detection capabilities. MAStitch leverages local and global perspectives, enabling comprehensive analysis of inter-agent interactions and intra-agent processes to detect anomalies. These perspectives are acquired by a pair of LLM-based agents: a Local Analyzer Agent (LAA), which evaluates each agent’s execution process, and a Global Analyzer Agent (GAA), which aggregates evidence from multiple agents to detect cross-agent failures. The GAA summarizes the anomalous patterns identified, classifies the entire execution log, and provides an explanation when threats are detected. We evaluate our method’s detection performance on six different MAS applications which were developed on two widely used MAS platforms. The evaluation results demonstrate MAStitch’s ability to detect a variety of threats, with an average F1 score of 0.9 and a minimal false positive rate of 0.078. © 2026 International Foundation for Autonomous Agents and Multiagent Systems.

[R0056] year=2025 | venue=EDPACS | type=Article
TITLE: EMPOWERING RED TEAMS WITH GENERATIVE AI: TRANSFORMING PENETRATION TESTING THROUGH ADAPTIVE INTELLIGENCE
KEYWORDS: artificial intelligence; numerical model; testing method; visualization
ABSTRACT: This paper investigates the integration of Generative Artificial Intelligence (GAI) and Large Language Models (LLMs) into Red Teaming exercises and penetration testing, addressing the growing complexity of cybersecurity threats and the limitations of traditional methods. Current Red Teaming practices often lack adaptability and efficiency in simulating sophisticated, evolving attack vectors, creating a gap in assessing real-world organizational vulnerabilities. This study introduces an AI-driven framework utilizing LLMs, such as ShellGPT, to enhance Red Teaming exercises by providing dynamic, context-aware suggestions across critical stages, from reconnaissance to exploitation, and even to the generation of the pentest report. Tested on a virtualized network mimicking real-world condition, LLMs demonstrated increased adaptability and improved the precision of attack simulations, enabling teams to emulate advanced adversarial techniques more effectively. The research also contributes a novel penetration testing model that integrates LLMs, addressing challenges such as operational scalability and sophisticated threat emulation. This approach not only elevates the quality of Red Teaming reports but also equips cybersecurity teams with advanced tools to anticipate and mitigate evolving threats. The findings underscore the transformative potential of GAI and LLMs in modernizing cybersecurity practices, ensuring they remain robust and aligned with the complexities of today’s threat landscape. © Copyright 2024 Taylor & Francis–All rights reserved.


